{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>You shall not steal, etc.</b> Scripture speaks of one who steals money, for behold it is said, ״וְלֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ בִשְׁמִי לַשָּׁקֶר״ — to say that if you stole, you will end up denying it, and if you denied it, you will end up swearing falsely. And one does not take an oath regarding denial of land and slaves, so it is certainly of one who steals money that Scripture speaks. Therefore it used the plural form, to warn also the one who shares with the thief, for he too hates his own soul, since ordinary money can be divided into shares. But one who steals a person — there is presumably nothing to divide into shares, even though he stands to sell him, for perhaps he will find no buyers. Since for the most part two people do not partner in kidnapping a person, therefore concerning it Scripture says (Exodus 20:13): ״לֹא תִּגְנֹב״ — in the singular. And likewise, ״לֹא תַעֲשֹׁק״ speaks of one who hires a laborer, and two people do not hire a single laborer in partnership; therefore it says ״לֹא תַעֲשֹׁק״ in the singular. And as for what is written, ״לֹא תַעֲשֹׁק אֶת רֵעֲךָ וְלֹא תִגְזֹל״ — whereas concerning the thief it did not mention ״רֵעֲךָ״ — this is because every thief steals also the awareness on high, and makes as if the Eye above does not see, as it were; and if so, he is not stealing from his fellow alone. But the robber oppresses only his fellow."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:31.695405844Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}